  <h2 id="what-are-lsas">What a Local Services Ad actually is</h2>
  <p>
    A Local Services Ad is Google’s “call a tradesman” box. It sits at the very top of the page,
    above the normal ads, above the map, above every website.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-serp-placement"
      alt="Mobile Google search showing Local Services Ads above normal Google Ads"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Top box: Local Services Ads. Below it: normal Google Ads. Everything else is further down.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>Three things make it different from every other ad you have been sold:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You only pay when someone calls or messages you.</strong> Not when they look. Not when
      they click.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Google vets you first.</strong> Licence, insurance, background check. Pass, and your ad
      carries a green “Google Guaranteed” tick that customers trust.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>There are no keywords to manage.</strong> You tell Google what jobs you do and where
      you work. It handles the rest.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The box does not appear on every search. In most US towns we see it on roughly half of
    electrician searches. When it does show, it owns the screen.
  </p>

  <h2 id="vs-other-channels">Why we turn this on before anything else</h2>
  <p>
    Every electrician we work with has been burned by advertising before. Yelp. Angi. Facebook. A
    “Google Ads guy” who spent $3,000 and sent four tyre-kickers.
  </p>
  <p>Here is how those stack up against Local Services Ads for an electrical shop:</p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-channel-comparison"
      alt="Table comparing Local Services Ads with Google Ads, social media, Yelp, and Angi"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Local Services Ads: cheapest, direct calls, nobody else gets the same lead.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    Normal Google Ads still have a place, but they are the expensive lane. You pay every time
    someone clicks, whether they call or not. We turn those on later, once Local Services Ads are
    already booking work.
  </p>
  <p>
    Shared lead sites like Angi sell the same homeowner to four electricians at once. You are racing
    three other shops to the phone. We do not recommend them.
  </p>

