      Billing, insurance, background check, Business Profile, reviews, budget, licence. All ticked
      means you are Google Guaranteed.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="profile-setup">Setting up your profile the right way</h2>
  <p>
    This is where most electricians leave money on the table. Google gives you a dozen settings and
    explains none of them. Here is what each one should be, and why.
  </p>

  <h3>1. Hours and your business highlights</h3>
  <p>
    Set your hours to <strong>the times someone will actually pick up the phone.</strong> Not the
    hours you would like to be open. If nobody answers at 7pm, do not list 7pm. A missed call hurts
    you more than a closed sign.
  </p>
  <p>
    Then pick six highlights for your ad. Choose the ones a homeowner cares about: licensed and
    insured, emergency service, free estimates, warranty on work. Only pick what is true.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-profile-hours-bio"
      alt="Local Services Ads profile page showing the hours schedule and business highlights"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>1: your ad only runs during the hours you set. 2: the highlights that appear on your ad.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>2. Budget and job types</h3>
  <p>
    Leave bidding on <strong>Automated</strong>. Then set your weekly budget higher than you expect
    to spend. That sounds backwards, so here is why.
  </p>
  <p>
    Google almost never spends your full budget on Local Services Ads. On our accounts it rarely
    reaches half. A high number tells Google “send me plenty,” and you can always lower it later.
    Just keep it under your card limit, because Google checks.
  </p>
  <p>
    For job types, <strong>tick everything you are willing to do.</strong> Panels, EV chargers,
    ceiling fans, outlets, the lot. If it is not ticked, Google will not send you that job.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-budget-job-types"
      alt="Local Services Ads budget set to automated bidding beside the list of electrical job types"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Automated bidding, a generous weekly budget, and every job type you can do turned on.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>3. Photos and message settings</h3>
  <p>
    Upload your logo and real photos of your crew doing real work. Homeowners want to see the
    person who is coming to their house. One van photo is plenty. Faces beat fleets.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turn <strong>Messages</strong> on. Yes, text leads are usually lower quality than phone calls.
    But Google rewards profiles that accept every kind of contact, and you will get more phone calls
    too.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-photos-settings"
      alt="Local Services Ads photo grid and settings with messages and bookings turned on"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Crew photos on the left. Messages, bookings, and brand search all switched on.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>4. Service area</h3>
  <p>
    Add every town or county you want work in, but keep it within about two hours’ drive of your
    base. Google knows where you are. Claiming half the state makes you look less local, and you
    drop down the list.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-service-areas-hours"
      alt="Local Services Ads service area map beside the business hours list"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Cover where you actually send trucks. Match the hours to when you answer.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2 id="hidden-score">The hidden score that decides who gets the calls</h2>
  <p>
    Here is what Google does not tell you. Every Local Services account has a score. You cannot see
    it. But it decides your position in that top box, and the electrician in position one gets the
    lion’s share of the calls.
  </p>
  <p>
    Worse: <strong>once that score drops badly, it almost never comes back.</strong> We have watched
    shops go from three calls a day to none and never recover on the same account.
  </p>

  <h3>The two things that break it for good</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Missed calls, or leaving people on hold.</strong> Google times how fast you pick up.
      Aim for under 30 seconds, nine calls out of ten. If your phone goes to voicemail during
      business hours, fix that before you spend a dollar.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ignored messages.</strong> Reply the same day, every time. Google compares your speed
      with the other electricians in your area.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>The smaller things that chip away at it</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>A budget set too low</li>
    <li>Service areas more than two hours away</li>
    <li>No photos, or half the settings left blank</li>
    <li>Messages switched off</li>
    <li>Leads with no name or notes (more on this next)</li>
    <li>Changing your budget every few days</li>
    <li>A weak Google Maps listing or a website that never shows up</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    That last one matters more than people think. Local Services Ads boost a shop that already looks
    good on Google. They rarely rescue one that does not.
  </p>

  <h2 id="manage-leads">The 5-minute daily habit that keeps calls coming</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part almost every electrician, and almost every agency, skips. It is also the main
    reason Hooked Up is still getting calls two years on.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every working day, open your leads and deal with each one. Here is exactly what that means.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-leads-menu"
      alt="Local Services Ads menu with Leads selected and a table of recent leads"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>
      Click Leads in the side menu. “Charged” means you paid for it. “Credited” means Google
      refunded it.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Step 1: Give every lead a name and a note</h3>
  <p>
    Click on the lead. Type the customer’s name and a quick note about the job: “panel upgrade,
    quoted Tuesday.” If you never got a name, write “Missed call” or “Price shopper.”
  </p>
  <p>
    Why bother? Because a lead with no name and no notes looks abandoned to Google. Enough of those
    and Google assumes you do not want the work.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-lead-detail-notes"
      alt="A Local Services Ads lead showing the message conversation and the notes field"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>A real reply, a name, and a note. This is what a healthy lead looks like.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Step 2: Mark it booked</h3>
  <p>
    Booked a date? Click <strong>Mark as booked → Upcoming</strong> and enter the day. Already been
    out, or they said no, or they just wanted a price? Click
    <strong>Mark as booked → Already complete</strong>.
  </p>
  <p>Either way, you are telling Google “this was a real electrical job.” That is what you want.</p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-mark-as-booked"
      alt="Local Services Ads lead page with the Mark as booked button highlighted"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Mark as booked, top right. Use it on every real electrical enquiry.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Step 3: Rate it, and be careful with Archive</h3>
  <p>
    When Google asks you to rate the lead, pick one of the “satisfied” options. You are not
    reviewing the customer. You are telling Google “send me more like this.”
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Only press Archive when the call was not electrical work at all.</strong> Wrong trade,
    spam, a salesman. If you archive real enquiries, even ones you lost, Google hears “send fewer,”
    and does.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-rate-lead"
      alt="Local Services Ads lead page with the Rate this lead link highlighted"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Rate this lead. Always positive for real electrical work.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h3>Step 4: When you win the job, add the price</h3>
  <p>
    Once a job is done, go back to the lead, hit <strong>Edit</strong>, and type in what you charged.
    Nobody else sees it. But Google now knows that a “panel upgrade” in that suburb turned into
    $2,800, and it goes looking for more of them.
  </p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-edit-booking-details"
      alt="Edit booking details window with a price estimate field on a completed lead"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>Edit → add the price. This is how Google learns which jobs make you money.</figcaption>
  </figure>
  <p>
    All of that takes about five minutes a day. Let it slide for more than three days and Google
    starts treating your account as asleep.
  </p>

  <h2 id="track-results">How to tell if you are winning</h2>
  <p>Open <strong>Reports</strong>. Three numbers matter.</p>
  <figure>
    <Picture
      name="lsa-reports-dashboard"
      alt="Local Services Ads Reports page showing spend, calls, and top position rate"
      sizes="(max-width: 720px) 100vw, 680px"
    />
    <figcaption>
      One real month: $2,387 spent, 131 phone calls, and the #1 spot 70% of the time.
    </figcaption>
  </figure>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Total spend and charged leads.</strong> Divide one by the other and you have your cost
      per call. In the month above, about $18.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>“Absolute top impression rate.”</strong> Plain English: how often you were the #1
      electrician in the box. Above 50% and you are winning your area.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cost per booked job.</strong> Google will not show you this. Keep a simple spreadsheet
      or tag LSA jobs in your job software. This is the number that pays the bills.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    If your spend is creeping up towards a quarter of your budget and you still want more calls,
    raise the budget. Otherwise leave it alone.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-goes-wrong">When things break, and how to fix them</h2>

  <h3>“My application has been pending for weeks”</h3>
  <p>
    Almost always a paperwork mismatch. Check your business name and address against your insurance
    and licence, fix any difference, then phone support and ask them to re-check.
  </p>

  <h3>“There is a red warning about billing”</h3>
  <p>
    When you sign up for Local Services Ads, Google quietly creates a normal Google Ads account
    behind it. Sometimes that account gets flagged. Log in at ads.google.com with the same email,
    clear the warning, and tell support you only use it for Local Services. This one can drag on for
    months if you leave it.
  </p>

  <h3>“I was getting calls every day and now I get none”</h3>
  <p>
    Open your leads and look at the dates. If the drop lines up with a stretch of missed calls or
    unanswered messages, your score took the hit. If you were new, start again properly. If you had
    been winning for months, be honest with yourself: the same account may never recover, and a
    fresh Business Profile plus a fresh Local Services account is sometimes the only way back.
  </p>

  <h3>“Calls are slowly drying up”</h3>
  <p>
    Check the small things: has your service area drifted too wide, did photos disappear, has the
    inbox sat untouched for a week? Then check your Google Maps listing. If a competitor has
    overtaken you on reviews and category, that pulls your ads down too.
  </p>

  <h2 id="google-ads-migration">The 2026 change: LSA is moving into Google Ads</h2>
  <p>
    Starting August 2026, Google began moving US electricians out of the separate Local Services
    dashboard and into the main Google Ads website. If you have not been moved yet, you will be.
    Google emails you about two weeks before.
  </p>

  <h3>What does not change</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>You still only pay when someone calls or messages</li>
    <li>Your ad still shows in the same top box on Google and Google Maps</li>
    <li>Your green tick carries over</li>
    <li>Everything in this guide about answering fast and managing leads still applies</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>What does change</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Your leads now live in Google Ads under <strong>Goals → Conversions → Leads</strong>
    </li>
    <li>Your weekly budget becomes a daily budget (Google divides it by seven)</li>
    <li>The option to set your own maximum price per lead goes away</li>
    <li>
      <strong>Your old reports disappear.</strong> Download or screenshot them before your move date.
      Your past leads and customer details do carry over.
